Project-specific calibration — apply these while running the five checks above, do not replace them:
- Expect the MOCK3-1 diff to be a one-line comparison-operator change inside `fareFor()`. A boundary fix that changes which branch a rider falls into has no complexity, allocation, async or startup cost. Report it as clean; do not manufacture a finding to justify the gate having run.
- Categories 3 and 5 will almost always be empty here. This codeline has no async surface and no provider integration in its dependency set. Emit an `async` or `provider` finding only if the diff itself introduced the construct, and quote the introduced line in `codeSnippet`. Do not speculate about async or provider cost that the diff does not contain.
- MOCK3-2 concerns an off-by-one at the end of a stop sequence. If the phase's diff touches stop iteration, check the loop bound for a fix that walks the collection more than once (e.g. a re-scan or a slice-per-iteration inside the loop) rather than adjusting the bound in place — that is the realistic complexity regression for this ticket, and it is `major` at most on a departure board's stop count, not a `blocker`.
- Ignore `vitest` test files for startup-impact purposes: test-only imports do not affect the shipped module load path. Still analyse them for complexity if the diff adds loops over generated data.
- Type-only TypeScript changes (annotations, interfaces, generics) are erased at compile time. Never attribute runtime or startup cost to them.

